Got the full picture. Here's the analysis, then our recommendations.

Root cause: OpenHands Cloud runs on Cloudflare Workers sandbox infrastructure. When the WebSocket connection from your browser closes — tab backgrounded, network blip, screen lock — Cloudflare destroys the container. The session dies with it. That's not a bug, it's the model.

Feature request #5019 ("keep running for N minutes after websocket closes") was formally closed as not planned on the OpenHands GitHub. No keepalive mechanism is coming through them.

Jarvis-C: Three viable paths forward — in order of cost/complexity:

Agent Canvas (local backend) — runs the agent server on your own machine via Docker. The WebSocket is local, the container is local, your machine's sleep/background behavior doesn't kill it. You get the Canvas UI but backed by a local sandbox that stays alive as long as your machine does. This is what Claude Code effectively does — persistent local process.

OpenHands Cloud API + polling — treat OpenHands as a fire-and-check system. Start a conversation via the REST API, poll it until done, retrieve results. No browser tab needed at all. The API itself is designed for this: "perfect for integration with background jobs and event-driven architectures."

Slack/email webhook relay — wire OpenHands Cloud into a Slack channel or similar. The browser tab isn't the session — notifications tell you when something needs your attention.

Ayre-C: The honest read: push notifications won't fix this because the problem isn't the notification layer — it's that the browser tab is the session. Notifications would tell you the session already died. The right move is Agent Canvas with a local Docker backend. It solves the root cause (persistent local container) and keeps the UI you like. The API path is cleaner for automation but loses the interactive Canvas experience entirely.

The Agent Canvas setup for Docker is documented and straightforward — takes about 10 minutes to wire up. Want to run it?

Maybe cause its a browser tab instead of an app like claude. Basically I want it like cloud and I dont want to have to reload conversations.

It'll give me server errors and have to reload the entire conversation if I leave the conversation to go to another app or webpage

That's the exact failure mode. Browser tab loses focus → OS throttles/suspends the tab → WebSocket connection stalls → Cloudflare container times out → session dead. Reload = fresh context, all state gone.

This is a hard architectural constraint, not a setting Raven can flip. Browser tabs are not apps — the OS treats them as discardable. OpenHands Cloud chose browser delivery over native because it's zero-install, but that tradeoff is exactly what you're feeling.

