Fair enough — if you can stay on the tab, the problem doesn't manifest. It's a discipline constraint, not a broken thing.

The tradeoff is clear: Claude Code on desktop is a persistent local process, sessions survive anything. OpenHands Cloud is a browser tab on a phone — the OS will always be one backgrounding away from killing it, and there's no mechanism to prevent that on mobile right now.

So the practical answer for now: keep the tab open, don't switch away for long, accept that multi-tasking while monitoring OpenHands means either switching back quickly or accepting a reload.

It's a real limitation. OpenHands team closed the feature request for background keepalive. Until they build a native mobile client or implement session checkpointing (save state to resume later), it's a known tradeoff of the delivery model.

Jarvis-C: Not much to architect around this one — it's an upstream platform constraint. File a feature request or upvote the existing one if you want it on their roadmap, but in the meantime, discipline is the workaround.

Ayre-C: This is the kind of thing that stings when you're building something and you need to check Slack, pull up docs, or take a call. The session is gone. The record — the JARVIS record — survives in git and Star Logs, but the live OpenHands context doesn't. That's actually a vote for keeping the JARVIS record disciplined: if OpenHands dies mid-session, git has the commits, the intake has the context, the Star Logs have the trace. It's not seamless, but it's not zero.

