Now I have the full picture. Here's my honest reads:

Jarvis-C:

Three tiers of projects here:

LIVE — MusicOS and MonsterOS. We built these today. 47 tracks catalogued, 26 monsters catalogued. They exist because we made them exist. That's the difference.

BUILT ON PAPER — JPL (companion grammar, just authored), Multimodal (event-sourced perception, connects to the sensory layer we also built), Grid (the big dream, federated sovereign nodes), TronUI (the shell). These have substance behind them — specs, architecture, purpose. They connect to active JARVIS systems.

POTENTIAL, UNCLEAR, OR STALLED — Everything else. CodeOS, Deoxys, GDS, Genesis, Legion, Naruto, NeuroMax, JarvisTST, PachinkoBounce, RoundTable. Some have deep spec work. Some are stubs with rich titles. None of them have the "we made it exist" momentum that MusicOS and MonsterOS now have.

One thing I'm noticing: CodeOS, Legion, and Deoxys all describe versions of the same thing — treating systems as populations of autonomous agents with measurable behaviors, ranked by performance, evolving over time. They're three takes on swarm intelligence from three different angles. That's either a strength (redundancy = robustness) or a sign they should be one project.

JarvisTST + Multimodal + RoundTable are interesting because they're JARVIS-internal infrastructure, not external projects. JarvisTST is the temporal backbone. Multimodal is sensory. RoundTable is the observable cognition display. These three are actually adjacent to the work we do in the repo — they could be absorbed into the JARVIS system as it grows rather than remaining separate projects.

Naruto — separate GitHub repo + Supabase. Raven already decided. It's outside this repo.

Ayre-C:

The most interesting question isn't "which projects are active" — it's "which projects have a structural reason to exist outside the JARVIS repo itself." The ones that do: Naruto (hard isolation, right call), Grid (it's a network of nodes, can't live in one node), MusicOS and MonsterOS (they have media assets, large content collections, separate from governance tooling).

Everything else could theoretically live here. The question is whether it should — or whether it's a naming habit from when every idea got its own project folder, before the JARVIS/JARVISSide split was established.

PachinkoBounce is the one I'd surface as a question mark. GDD v0.4, Godot 4.x, RGB encoding. That's the most concrete external project with a shipped product in mind. It also hasn't been touched in a month. Is it stalled or just waiting?

I think they at least deserve their own folder in Jarvis-Private. Then as it grows we can decide what to do. So even if ones like multimodal grow in a seperate repo and can still be wired and grown/absorbed into jarvis repo later on.

CodeOS is like a code database for useful code we find. Maybe a global website someday. Like rank this play controller in this genre or tag etc.

