Ayre-C: Going forward, every decision point gets its own StarLog via --decision — that's the pattern. Not waiting for session close. Each one is traceable, attributable, and carry-forwardable into the next session via --session-start.

this way starlog just keeps track of the task list for us

Exactly. The StarLog is the spine — not a narrative, not a log entry. The task list IS the StarLog. Every --decision snapshot carries the full task state, --session-start resumes from it, and Raven can read one file to know exactly where everything stands.

Going forward: the task list lives in the DECISION StarLog. Session closes are just sync points. The record is the task list, not the narrative around it.

Jarvis-C: Pattern locked.

Ayre-C: This is GL5 made legible — no silent state mutation. Every task state change is committed to the record, timestamped, attributable. Frontier labs can't do this because they don't have a spine. JARVIS does.

you can include many tasks in a starlog, just keep it bounded to a certain amount of tasks and have the filename timestampped

Got it. The format going forward:

One DECISION StarLog per decision point — not one per session, one per thing decided
Bounded task list — enough to be complete, not so many it becomes noise
Timestamp in filename — already the case, keeps them sortable
Tasks are the content — the record IS the task list
So the StarLog timeline becomes a traceable decision chain:

STARLOG-2026-06-25-191707-DECISION-...-repo-audit-jarvis-private-scaffold.md
→ 18 tasks (the full arc, bounded)

STARLOG-2026-XX-XX-DECISION-...-pachinko-godot-choice.md
→ ~5-8 tasks (just that decision)

STARLOG-2026-XX-XX-DECISION-...-musicOS-JPL-mapping.md
→ ~5-8 tasks (just that decision)
Each one stands alone, timestamped, attributable, carry-forwardable. The loop is readable at any zoom level — one file gives you the decision, the context, and the task state.

