and you dont have to build right away, we can have a jam sesh first

Exactly. Let's talk through it first.

So far the core loop is: tap to spawn balls → brick breaker physics → collect RGB orbs → balls shift RGB encoding → stat distributions change → different playstyles emerge.

But the real depth is the RGB sub-channels stacking:

Ball has dominant RGB → base color + main stat distribution
STR sub-channel → inner core color: SIZE (red) / MASS (green) / DAMAGE (blue)
TEQ sub-channel → outer ring color: BOUNCE (yellow) / SPEED (cyan) / MULTI (magenta)
Rarity → stat multiplier + name tier
Texture + Pattern → visual variety
RGB orbs collected → shift which RGB channel is dominant → ball identity changes mid-game
So a ball isn't just "a red ball." It's: Epic RGB / STR:22 MASS-DMG / TEQ:14 BNC+SPD / pulse pattern / gold outer ring / blue core. That's a piece of gear. That's the Borderlands loot loop applied to every ball.

The vampire survivors layer: balls accumulate, combos build, you keep tapping to maintain ball count. When balls die you lose RGB encoding momentum. The question isn't just "do I have balls" — it's "do I have the RIGHT balls with the right STR/TEQ balance."

What does the game feel like to you? What's the fantasy — are you a ball architect designing the perfect payload? Or are you reacting to the chaos, collecting what you can, building combos?

if everything has itd own rgb channel, we can do a lot of funky stuff

Exactly. Everything is a channel. Everything has R/G/B. Everything interacts.

What each channel could do across systems:

System R (Power) G (Rhythm) B (Range)
Balls Damage + knockback Bounce + timing Chain + area
Bricks Hard + slow HP Fast respawn Shatters into pieces
Orbs +VIG/STR drop +END/DEX drop +INT/ARC drop
Enemies (future) Melee/charge Sweep/pattern Ranged/aoe
Arena Walls pulse Floor tempo Gravity field
UI bar Red glow frame Green combo ring Blue multiplier
Combos R×R = crit G×G = rhythm chain B×B = chain explosion
R×G Power+Rhy = super bounce
R×B Power+Rng = explode on hit
G×B Rhythmn+Rng = area pulse
R×G×B Full spectrum = legendary effect
Bricks could have their own RGB that determines what orb type they drop — a red brick gives you a red orb, a green brick gives you a green orb. And if you hit a brick with a ball of the same RGB, you get a resonance bonus. Matching colors = amplified effect.

Or: bricks have RGB weaknesses. Red balls do extra damage to blue bricks. Green balls bounce higher off red bricks. The arena becomes a puzzle of RGB matching.

The environment could shift based on your ball composition too — if you have mostly red balls, the arena pulses red. Mostly blue, the gravity shifts outward. The world reacts to what you're carrying.

And textures/patterns aren't just aesthetic — maybe a swirled texture means the ball has a spiral physics effect. A pulsing pattern means it triggers a shockwave on brick kill. The visual IS the mechanic.

This is basically an RGB operating system applied to a game. Every element is a signal AND a function. That's the depth.

What do you want to jam on first — the ball system, the brick system, or the combo/chain system?

youre a ball collector. finding new and crazy balls

That's the fantasy. You're not playing the game — you're curating the chaos. Watching your collection do work. The ball found. The rare drop. The legendary.

Core loop:

Tap to spawn — ball joins the field, does its thing
Watch — balls are autonomous, they bounce, kill, chain on their own
Collect orbs — shifts RGB dominance → balls change identity mid-game
Rare drops — brick kills sometimes drop a NEW ball (the gacha moment)
Loop ends — balls fall, you restart with your knowledge of what worked
The satisfying moment: a Mythic ball spawns from a brick kill, it has the right STR/TEQ balance, it starts chaining, the combo climbs, you stop tapping and just WATCH. That's the thing. You're not clicking fast — you're watching your collection do the thing.

The permanent progression: track unique balls collected. Over runs, you see more rare drops because your total ball count grows. The collection grows.

Questions for the jam:

Spawn cost — free (tap anywhere), or limited (limited balls at a time, tap to replace)?
Ball lifespan — infinite, or bricks eventually kill them? Or balls "die" after a timer?
Rare drop rate — when a brick dies, what's the chance it spawns a new ball? Rarity of the dropped ball tied to brick type?
What makes a ball "good" — STR/TEQ/DEX/RGB sub-channels all interact. Should some combinations be busted/fun rather than balanced?
Field size — 16:9 phone portrait, like a pinball trench? Or wider arena?
Let's lock the core fantasy and build around it.

limited balls most definitely, thats how we create stakes. all bricks must die by balls. and some balls are good from extra speed or mass or multiplying or elements or mass or gravity etc. whatever physics and systems we have access to we can use. a major nort star is it has to look good and feel good. like the ps5 dualsense. we must feel the balls. fun addictive game from pure spectacle, physics, collecting, and vibrational feel

Limited balls. Bricks die from balls only. Balls die from falling off screen. Every ball is a precious resource you're managing. That's the stakes.

North star: DualSense feel. Haptic. The screen shakes when a Mythic ball chains. The camera micropulses on a combo spike. The RGB bar pulses when you're in resonance. You FEEL the collection.

