On StarLog tracking:

The StarLog (audit/starlogs/) is the session record — it's committed to git and forms the continuity layer. This session's GOVERNANCE StarLog is written and committed.

What it captures:

What was done (the 9 decisions above)
The result (GREEN — 242)
What wasn't addressed and why
What it doesn't capture: per-change granularity. That's what git log is for. The StarLog is the compressed synthesis — the decisions worth remembering at session close, not the step-by-step. That's the right grain for a companion that grows through time.

scripts/sl.py handles session-start (sync from last committed SL) and session-close (write + commit). The GL5 event discipline is at the decision level in the StarLog, not individual file edits. That's the correct split.

