what is branch protection and do we need it for the Jarvis-Private

What it does:

Branch protection on main means:

No force-push to main
No direct pushes to main at all — everything goes through a PR
PRs require at least 1 approval from a code owner
CI checks (if configured) must pass before merge
Admins can't bypass either — enforces it even for repo owners
Without it, anyone (or any automation) with push access can push directly to main — no review, no trace, no gate.

For Jarvis-Private — honest assessment:

It's your private repo. You're the only one with access. The question is whether you want to enforce a review discipline on yourself, or trust yourself to not push red directly to main.

Arguments for it on a private repo:

Catches CI failures before they hit main if you wire status checks
Forces PRs → creates a reviewable history even for solo work
Protects against accidental force-pushes wiping history
Aligns both repos under the same governance model
Arguments against on a private repo:

Adds friction for a single-user repo
No one else is reviewing — approval from "code owner" means approving your own code
You could always just... not push directly
My read: JARVIS (public) absolutely needs it — it's the production system and CI gates matter. Jarvis-Private is personal scaffolding — you can govern it by habit without the mechanical gate. The value is lower there.

Recommendation: Skip it on Jarvis-Private unless you're running automated agents against it that you want constrained by CI. GL2 (no autonomous self-mod) covers most of what branch protection would add.

