we can at least keep the firewall as an idea/project. and whats the jfspatch-0002

JFSPatch-0002 — a patch file (2660 bytes) in JarvisMain/Patches/. Written as a governance spec for system resilience and traceability. The content:

Change classification — every change must be labeled STRUCTURAL / SEMANTIC / IDENTITY / EXECUTION / OBSERVATIONAL
Trace ID standard — every action emits a record with JNL, timestamp, source, type, diff ref, CHAOS ref
Drift detection — mismatches between JD meaning and CHAOS behavior, JFS structure and JNL routing, etc.
Reconstruction protocol — if the system breaks, recover in order: HADES → CHAOS → JD → JFS → JNL
Versioning rule — every change increments the relevant layer version
Boundary rule — JFS / JD / JNL / CHAOS are strictly separate; cross-layer mutation needs JARVIS mediation
Core truth stack — the 5 things required for any rebuild: JNL + JD + JFS + CHAOS + JARVIS
My assessment: ~80% of this is already implemented — GL5 (event discipline), HUGINN (event logging), validate.py (drift detection), Yggdrasil rebuild spec, seq-registry (versioning), JFS/JD/JNL separation. What's not wired: deterministic replay from CHAOS, LIMINAL mode (mentioned but doesn't exist), and explicit JARVIS mediation for cross-layer changes.

The patch is essentially a design document for what the Yggdrasil substrate already delivers. It's not dead code — it's a reference spec that could be formally archived or updated to reflect the current implementation.

