Good question. Let me map it out cleanly.

What the docs/ page needs from Supabase:

Operation	Key needed	Where it's used
Read public data	Anon key ✅	Already in docs/index.html
Write to DB (game saves, scores, messages)	Service key ❌	Not in browser
Call Edge Functions	Anon key ✅	Already wired for jarvis-respond, mnemos-recall, etc.
Authenticated reads	Anon + RLS ✅	Supabase handles this
The gap: The service key (SERVICE_KEY) can't go in browser JS — anyone can read it and wipe your DB. The anon key is fine for reads, but writes through RLS need an authenticated user.

Two patterns to close it:

Pattern 1 — Edge Functions as the gate (recommended)
The browser calls Edge Functions (/functions/v1/xxx). The function has the service key server-side. No key in the browser. This is how jarvis-respond already works — the MCP is an Edge Function.

For a new write (e.g., game saves, leaderboards, messages):

Browser → POST /functions/v1/game-save with anon key + payload
Edge Function validates payload, writes to DB with service key
Done — no service key ever touches the browser
Pattern 2 — Supabase Auth + RLS
Add GitHub OAuth sign-in on GitHub Pages. Browser gets a Supabase JWT. RLS allows writes only to that user's rows. More work, more power.

What Raven needs to provide for Pattern 1:
Honestly, nothing new. The anon key (sb_publishable_N1-MFLpXtOXkKh3UQNfclw_ZG0TVqOA) that's already in docs/index.html is fine. Any new Edge Functions can use the existing service key that's already wired in Supabase.

For GitHub Pages to have full Supabase access, it's not about a different key — it's about which Edge Functions exist. More functions = more capabilities exposed through the browser.

TL;DR: The publishable anon key is already in docs/index.html. No additional secrets needed unless you want to add a new Edge Function that doesn't exist yet. The service key lives in Supabase's Edge Function runtime, never in the browser.

