how does lfs work. also you can talk to copilot on github now and that has ai too which is cool

GitHub LFS (Large File Storage):

Think of it like a pointer system. Instead of storing the 130MB file in git, git stores a small text file that says "this file lives at https://git-lfs.github.com/...". When you git clone, git downloads the real file from LFS servers on demand.

Regular git:     130MB file stored directly in the repo history
LFS git:         1KB pointer file in repo, 130MB on LFS servers
Setup is 3 commands:

git lfs install          # one-time: enable LFS globally
git lfs track "*.zip.*"  # tell LFS which files to handle
git commit               # LFS uploads automatically on push
No separate upload step, no manual management. After git push, the files live on LFS servers. You get 5GB free on public repos.

GitHub Copilot integration — tell me more.

