can the retroarch page be fed into the gameboy io screen on https://hurrisonferd.github.io/jarvis/

Good question. Let me check the GameBoy IO screen structure:

Find GB screen rendering
Check libretro canvas options
Check Module setup
Check canvas in player
The RetroArch player runs in an iframe (ra-frame) with its own canvas (#canvas). The parent page's #emu-canvas is separate. Direct pixel streaming between iframes isn't possible without a shared buffer.

The current setup already puts RetroArch in the IO aesthetic — the scanline overlay wraps the iframe, and it fills the screen. But here's what's achievable:

Option A — Status bar integration (simple)
The openRetroArch() overlay is already styled and overlaid on the IO page. RetroArch renders inside it. The retro overlay IS the IO screen's emulator mode.

Option B — RetroArch as a panel (no iframe)
Load RetroArch JS directly into the main page's DOM. This lets RetroArch render into #emu-canvas directly. But: RetroArch needs SharedArrayBuffer (COI headers), which the service worker provides for the iframe but not for inline JS.

Option C — Iframe with IO chrome (medium)
Style the iframe wrapper to have the IO header/footer so it looks like it's part of the interface rather than a full takeover.

The cleanest path that actually works: RetroArch stays in the iframe, we give it an IO-flavored wrapper — IO header showing "RETROARCH" + core name, IO footer with controls. The scanline overlay already does the aesthetic.

Want me to add the IO chrome wrapper around the iframe, so it feels like the RetroArch screen IS the IO screen? Something like:

┌─ RETROARCH ──────────── [✕ CLOSE] ─┐
│                                    │
│      (RetroArch UI here)           │
│                                    │
└────────────────────────────────────┘
That's essentially what happens now with the scanline overlay — just with the browser's default frame around the HTML. I can strip that with scoped CSS injected into the service worker, or just accept the current look since it's already styled.

