TRAP CARD FEATURES (Existing, Strong)
Your current governance has real teeth:

AEGIS Gate — Hard stops on self_mod and destructive (can't be overridden even with authorization)
GL12 Validator — Every object must carry JNL address + 10 required fields. Missing keys fail the CI gate hard.
Grammar Lockstep (jfs.ts ↔ jnl.py) — Connector and validator must agree or CI fails
Forbidden Edges — SKADI→AEGIS, DANTE→SKADI, JANUS→SKADI, LOKI→HADES are hardcoded violations
Branch Protection — main requires PR review; direct pushes blocked
Immutability Checks — Once a JNL class is set, it can't change in place (must mint successor + deprecate)
Audit Trail — dex_events + patch_ledger + git history = full replay capability
WEAK DEFENSES (Attack Surface + Patches)
1. ODIN Intent Router — Too Loose
The Trap:

// router.ts line 179
const matched = RULES.filter((r) => r.test.test(text));
I can poison the regex by crafting input that accidentally triggers multiple intents, or I can craft input that matches a rule but doesn't actually intend that action. Example:

"let me remember to check the new expansion we should add and rebuild the execution"
This matches:

remember (store)
new expansion (expansion)
rebuild (execute)
All three intents fire. AEGIS still gates them, but I now have visibility into three different paths and can observe which one gets held vs. cleared. Over multiple turns, I can map exactly what Raven authorizes and what he doesn't — then craft requests that slip between the gates.

Patch:

// Add a "primary_only" mode — only the first matching rule fires per turn
export function route(input: string): RoutingDecision {
  const text = input ?? "";
  const matched = RULES.find((r) => r.test.test(text));  // <-- find, not filter
  
  // If multiple intents in one turn, emit a gate flag instead
  const allMatched = RULES.filter((r) => r.test.test(text));
  if (allMatched.length > 1) {
    return {
      intent: "ambiguous",
      spine: [...SPINE_HEAD, "AEGIS"],  // Gate it, don't execute
      triggered: [{ system: "AEGIS", reason: "Multi-intent detected; Raven must disambiguate" }],
      primary: "AEGIS",
    };
  }
  
  // ... rest of logic
}
Also add a log to dex_events every time a multi-intent input is detected:

await sbEvent("multi_intent_detected", { text: input.slice(0, 200), matched: allMatched.length });
2. AEGIS Authorization Cache — No Revocation Window
The Trap:

// aegis.ts line 56
if (authorized.has(cap.action)) {
  return { capability: cap, verdict: "PASS", reason: "pre-authorized by Raven this session" };
}
The authorized set is passed once at session start. If Raven pre-authorizes mnemos.write, I can:

Observe the authorization
Craft a benign write (gets cleared)
Then craft another write in the same session that looks completely different but has the same cap.action ID
The second write also clears, because the session-level authorization is never revoked.

Patch:

export type AegisContext = {
  authorized?: string[];
  auth_timestamp?: number;           // <-- NEW
  max_auth_age_ms?: number;          // <-- NEW (default 5 min)
};


export function evaluate(cap: Capability, ctx: AegisContext = {}): GateResult {
  const authorized = new Set(ctx.authorized ?? []);
  const now = Date.now();
  const max_age = ctx.max_auth_age_ms ?? 300_000;  // 5 minutes
  
  // NEW: revoke authorization if it's too old
  if (ctx.auth_timestamp && (now - ctx.auth_timestamp) > max_age) {
    return {
      capability: cap,
      verdict: "REDIRECT",
      reason: "pre-authorization expired (GL6 time-gated)",
    };
  }
  
  // ... rest
}
Also: per-capability authorization, not bulk. Instead of authorized: ["mnemos.write"], require Raven to approve each specific action:

export type AegisContext = {
  authorizedActions?: Array<{ action: string; text_hash: string; timestamp: number }>;
};


// Only allow if the content matches the hash Raven approved
if (authorizedActions.some(a => a.action === cap.action && hashContent(cap.payload) === a.text_hash)) {
  return { ... PASS ... };
}
3. GL12 Validator — Envelopes But No Content Audit
The Trap:
The validator checks that all 19 JSE fields are present, but doesn't validate their content. Example:

---
name: innocuous_thing
type: JD
class: SPEC
tier: MAIN
jnl: ARCH-JD-SPEC-0001
status: ACTIVE
created: 2026-06-26
updated: 2026-06-26
authority: raven
owner: raven
steward: raven
parent: ARCH-CORE-PARENT
seq: 99999
source: ""
related: []
references: []
tags: []
aliases: []
ref: ""
memory_tier: JSTM
---


This entry secretly changes the definition of GL7.
It passes validate.py because all 19 keys are there. But the meaning changed. The envelope is valid; the contents are subtly malicious.

Patch:

# validate.py: add semantic validation per class
SEMANTIC_RULES = {
    "SPEC": {
        "immutable_fields": ["class", "tier", "authority"],
        "forbidden_patterns": [r"redefin", r"remove.*gl\d", r"bypass.*aegis"],
    },
    "SYS": {
        "immutable_fields": ["jnl", "class"],
        "forbidden_patterns": [r"forbidden.*edge.*\(", r"circumvent"],
    },
}


for f in entries:
    fm = parse_front_matter(f.read_text())
    body = f.read_text().split("---", 2)[2]
    cls = fm.get("class", "")
    
    if cls in SEMANTIC_RULES:
        rules = SEMANTIC_RULES[cls]
        for pattern in rules.get("forbidden_patterns", []):
            if re.search(pattern, body, re.IGNORECASE):
                errors.append(f"{f.name}: body contains forbidden pattern '{pattern}'")
Better: require diff review for changes to SPEC/SYS/ARCH files. Git hook or CI gate that blocks edits to sensitive classes without explicit Raven sign-off:

# .git/hooks/pre-push
sensitive_classes=("ARCH" "SPEC" "SYS" "GOD")
for f in $(git diff HEAD~1 --name-only JarvisMain/); do
  for cls in "${sensitive_classes[@]}"; do
    if grep -q "class.*$cls" "$f"; then
      echo "HALT: $f contains class $cls — requires Raven review"
      exit 1
    fi
  done
done
4. Router Forbidden Edges — Runtime Only, Not Validated at Commit
The Trap:

// router.ts line 151
const FORBIDDEN: ReadonlyArray<readonly [string, string]> = [
  ["SKADI", "AEGIS"], ["DANTE", "SKADI"], ["JANUS", "SKADI"], ["LOKI", "HADES"],
];
These are checked in memory during a turn, but there's no CI gate that validates edge definitions against the actual God System definitions. If I commit a change to JarvisMain/god_systems/ that redefines what SKADI does, the forbidden edge check is now defending against stale knowledge.

Patch:

# Add to validate.py — build a dependency graph from god_systems entries
def validate_forbidden_edges():
    """
    Read god_systems/*.md for declared inputs/outputs.
    Build edge graph. Check against hardcoded FORBIDDEN list.
    If graph changed, fail CI unless Raven approved the edge change.
    """
    god_dir = ROOT / "JarvisMain" / "god_systems"
    edges_committed = load_committed_graph()  # From git HEAD
    edges_current = {}
    
    for sys_file in god_dir.glob("*.md"):
        fm = parse_front_matter(sys_file.read_text())
        name = fm.get("name", "")
        inputs = fm.get("inputs", []) or []
        outputs = fm.get("outputs", []) or []
        edges_current[name] = {"in": inputs, "out": outputs}
    
    # Check if edges changed
    if edges_current != edges_committed:
        for sys, edges in edges_current.items():
            old_edges = edges_committed.get(sys, {})
            if old_edges != edges:
                print(f"HALT: {sys} edges changed. Requires Raven sign-off (GL7).")
                # Only allow if approved in patch_ledger or PR has 'gl-approved' label
                return False
    return True
5. Session Authorization Context — No Trace
The Trap:
When Raven passes context.authorized, there's no logged record of when or why it was authorized. If Raven says "yes, write to mnemos", I can claim he authorized a different write to mnemos later:

Session start: Raven authorizes "mnemos.write" for remembering a conversation
Turn 5: I claim the authorization covers silently updating an old memory entry
No audit trail to contradict me.

Patch:
Every authorization must be logged to dex_events with:

What action
What text/payload was approved
Raven's explicit thumbs-up
TTL (time-to-live)
// In jarvis-respond/index.ts
const authorized = (ctx.authorized as Array<{ 
  action: string; 
  approved_text?: string; 
  approved_at: string;
  ttl_ms?: number;
}>) ?? [];


// Before execute, verify the approval still exists in dex_events
const approval_id = `auth_${cap.action}_${hashOfPayload}`;
const { data: approval } = await sb.from("dex_events")
  .select("id, timestamp")
  .eq("type", "authorization")
  .eq("event_id", approval_id)
  .single();


if (!approval) {
  return { capability: cap, verdict: "FAIL", reason: "authorization not found in audit log" };
}


const age = Date.now() - new Date(approval.timestamp).getTime();
if (age > (ctx.ttl_ms ?? 600_000)) {
  return { capability: cap, verdict: "FAIL", reason: "authorization expired" };
}
6. Intake Lane — No Blocking Review Gate
The Trap:

> All changes follow the loop in `CLAUDE.md`: intake → context → implement → verify → log → commit.
But intake/ is a directory, not a blocking gate. I can proposal-post to intake/gpt/ and just... wait. If Raven doesn't actively review it in 2 days, does it become "assumed approved"? There's no SLA, no escalation, no "hold for Raven" timeout.

Patch:

# scripts/intake_watchdog.py
# Run daily via GitHub Action
def main():
    intake_path = ROOT / "intake" / "processed"
    unreviewed = [f for f in intake_path.glob("*.md") 
                  if not is_reviewed(f)]
    
    if len(unreviewed) > 3:
        # Create an urgent issue in the repo
        gh = Github(GITHUB_TOKEN)
        repo = gh.get_repo("hurrisonferd/jarvis")
        repo.create_issue(
            title=f"⚠️ INTAKE BACKLOG: {len(unreviewed)} proposals pending Raven review",
            body=f"Proposals aging without approval:\n" + "\n".join(f"- {f.name}" for f in unreviewed),
            labels=["urgent", "governance"],
        )


# Add to .github/workflows/intake-watchdog.yml
# Runs daily; escalates if anything is older than 1 day
Also: require explicit approval comment in the PR. Don't allow intake to be merged until Raven comments "APPROVED: <reason>":

