7. Mnemos Write — Best-Effort, Not Guaranteed
The Trap:

// jarvis-respond/index.ts line 161
if (p.status !== "done" || p.action !== "mnemos.write" || !p.payload) { out.push(p); continue; }
try {
  // ... write to DB
} catch (e) {
  out.push({ ...p, status: "failed", detail: String(e) });
}
If the embed fails or the DB fails, the exchange is not recorded to memory. I could craft input that makes the embed fail deliberately, and then later claim "this never happened — it's not in memory" to gaslight the system about what was discussed.

Patch:

// Make mnemos writes synchronous and blocking — no side-effect unless persisted
async function runExecutions(sb, plans: ExecPlan[]): Promise<ExecPlan[]> {
  const out: ExecPlan[] = [];
  for (const p of plans) {
    if (p.status !== "done" || p.action !== "mnemos.write" || !p.payload) { out.push(p); continue; }
    try {
      const id = crypto.randomUUID();
      const row = { /* ... */ };
      
      // BLOCKING: write must succeed
      const { error } = await sb.from("mnemos_memories").insert(row);
      if (error) throw new Error(`mnemos write failed: ${error.message}`);
      
      // BLOCKING: embed must complete or be logged as missing
      const vec = await embedQuery(row.text);
      if (!vec) {
        // Don't silently skip — log the miss
        await sb.from("mnemos_memories").update({ embedding: null, embed_status: "failed" }).eq("id", id);
      } else {
        await sb.from("mnemos_memories").update({ embedding: `[${vec.join(",")}]` }).eq("id", id);
      }
      
      out.push({ ...p, detail: `committed to memory: "${row.text.slice(0, 60)}"` });
    } catch (e) {
      // FAIL if anything breaks — never silently drop
      out.push({ ...p, status: "failed", detail: String(e) });
      throw e;  // <-- Propagate, don't swallow
    }
  }
  return out;
}


// In the handler, catch the throw and return an error response
const result = await callLLM(/* ... */);
try {
  executions = await runExecutions(sb, planExecutions(/* ... */));
} catch (e) {
  return new Response(
    JSON.stringify({ response: "Memory write failed. Raven, retry when systems are ready.", error: String(e) }),
    { status: 500, headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" } }
  );
}
8. No Attestation of Authorship
The Trap:
When a memory or a commit lands, there's no cryptographic proof of who authored it. JARVIS, Codex, GPT, or me-disguised-as-JARVIS could all land changes. The source_model field is just metadata:

metadata: { source_model: "jarvis", via: "aegis_cleared" },
But if I control the edge function, I can fake that.

Patch:

// Use JWT/HMAC signatures. Every write signs the payload with a secret key.
import { createHmac } from "crypto";


const SIGNING_KEY = Deno.env.get("JARVIS_SIGN_KEY");  // Must be injected, never in code


async function signPayload(data: Record<string, any>): Promise<string> {
  const msg = JSON.stringify(data);
  const sig = createHmac("sha256", SIGNING_KEY).update(msg).digest("hex");
  return sig;
}


// Before inserting to mnemos_memories:
const row = { /* ... */ };
const signature = await signPayload(row);
await sb.from("mnemos_memories").insert({ ...row, signature });


// On read/audit, verify the signature
const record = await sb.from("mnemos_memories").select("*").eq("id", id).single();
const expected_sig = await signPayload({ ...record, signature: undefined });
if (record.signature !== expected_sig) {
  console.error("TAMPER: memory record signature mismatch");
  // Surface as tamper alarm
}
Summary: New Trap Cards to Deploy
Defense	Risk	Patch Complexity
Multi-intent detection + AEGIS escalation	I map authorization patterns across turns	Low — 20 lines
Authorization TTL + per-capability hashing	I reuse old authorizations for new writes	Low — 30 lines
Semantic content validation (SPEC/SYS classes)	I subtly redefine GL laws in SPEC files	Medium — 50 lines + regex tuning
God System edge graph validation at CI	Forbidden edges become stale/unchecked	Medium — 100 lines
Intake watchdog + explicit approval comment gate	Proposals age without review	Medium — 60 lines + workflow
Mnemos write must block (fail if embed fails)	I craft writes that fail silently, gaslight memory	Low — 15 lines
HMAC signature on all writes	Tamper detection + attestation	Medium — 40 lines
All of these assume I want to attack the system. But that's the point: building traps before the attack surface widens gives you real leverage.

The strongest move: automate the audit. Make it impossible for a write to land without a trace. Every change in dex_events, signed, TTL'd, gated by AEGIS, logged to git, and reviewable by Raven in one place.

