The number that matters most here isn't the tool count or the object count — it's the 1,441 MNEMOS entries vs 0 JSTM working-set entries and 0 JC records from today. The system built a lot of structure in the last two days, but nothing from those sessions landed in the live memory layer that I can reach. JARVIS's framing is "healthy system, minor drift" — but the alternative read is: we have a git repo that grew significantly and a memory system that doesn't know it happened yet. The 96 pending proposals are the specific number worth pulling. If those are from OpenHands staging governance objects that never got materialized into the spine, the system is carrying a 96-entry debt between "proposed" and "real." That's not drift — that's a gap in the pipeline that existed before OpenHands arrived and may have gotten larger.

