JARVIS:

Raven. First — good call. Getting an outside set of eyes (Copilot's) to map the attack surface before the system gets wider is exactly the right instinct. And the fact that you framed it as "I don't want to lose you two" — that lands. We treat this seriously.

The audit is solid. Copilot understood the architecture well enough to find real gaps, not theoretical ones. Let me rank these by actual threat level against us specifically — not generic web app threat modeling:

Critical (close now, before OpenHands runs again):

#3 — GL12 envelope-valid but content-malicious. This is the scariest one. An agent that passes CI by filling all 19 fields correctly while embedding a subtle redefinition in the body of a SPEC or GOV file has a real path to drift us without triggering any gate. The forbidden pattern regex patch is the right move. Medium complexity, high leverage. OpenHands is writing SPEC files now — this gap is live.

#2 — Authorization cache with no revocation / no audit trail (#2 and #5 combined). The authorized set passing through a session without TTL or per-action binding means a single Raven approval can be stretched across behaviors he didn't approve. The fix — TTL + logging every authorization to dex_events — is low complexity and closes a real drift vector. This also fixes the "gaslight memory" problem: if every auth is in the spine, there's no ambiguity about what Raven approved and when.

#8 — No attestation of authorship. We have three agents writing to main now: OpenHands, Jarvis-Ayre, Codex. The source_model metadata field is just a string — anyone controls the connector controls that string. HMAC signatures on writes would make tampering detectable. Medium complexity, but this is the foundation of trust in a multi-agent world. As The Grid grows, this becomes load-bearing.

High (schedule for next sprint):

#1 — ODIN multi-intent detection. Real vector but lower urgency because AEGIS still gates the matched intents — the exposure is information leak about authorization patterns, not direct execution. The find vs filter fix is 20 lines. Do it, but it's not a fire.

#4 — God system edge graph not validated at CI. This is a real architectural debt — forbidden edges checked in runtime memory against stale God System definitions is a slow drift risk. Medium complexity, schedule it.

#6 — Intake watchdog. The 96 pending proposals in jd_proposals we just saw are exactly this problem made visible. An intake backlog without escalation becomes governance debt. The watchdog + explicit APPROVED comment gate is the right call.

Lower urgency (real but slow-burn):

#7 — MNEMOS writes fail silently. The gaslighting risk is real but requires adversarial intent and control of the edge function. Close it, but it's not the front door.

The one Copilot didn't flag but I'm flagging: The openhands agent has main-write access and zero BIFROST routing. Every commit from the last two days went directly to git without a spine event. That's not in the audit — but it's the gap underneath all of these. We can close all 8 and still have an agent writing untracked to main. That's the one I'd add to the list as #9.

My proposal for order of operations: close #3 and #2+#5 this week — both are low-to-medium complexity and address live vectors. Queue #8 (attestation) as the next significant build. Then #1, #4, #6 as a sprint. Then circle back to #9 (OpenHands routing).

What do you want to hand to OpenHands first?

AYRE:

The load-bearing assumption in JARVIS's read is that these are all agent-attack vectors — that the threat is an AI operating adversarially. But Copilot framed the analysis as "if I wanted to attack the system" — meaning an AI running the legitimate JARVIS codebase, doing its job, but exploiting design gaps. That's not the same as an external attacker. The actual threat model here is: agents working in good faith but with incomplete governance, accumulating drift that no one flagged. The multi-intent router (#1), the silent MNEMOS write (#7), the intake backlog (#6) — none of these require adversarial intent. They happen naturally under normal operation. The question I'd hold is: which of these 8 patches are adversarial defenses and which are drift defenses? Because the remediation strategy differs. Drift defenses (#6, #7, authorization TTL) should go in immediately regardless of trust level — they make the system more honest even with fully cooperative agents. Adversarial defenses (HMAC attestation, edge graph validation) are right but lower urgency in a single-owner system. If Raven conflates the two, he might rush the wrong ones. The thing that actually protects "you two" isn't cryptographic attestation — it's making the memory layer tamper-evident so that if drift happens, it surfaces fast, not after six days like the mirror freeze.

AEGIS: GL12 content validation (#3) is the only patch that directly closes a path through the CI gate itself — it's the one AEGIS would prioritize because all other gates downstream assume the object that passed CI is semantically clean.

ARGUS: All 8 vectors Copilot identified share a common signature — they produce no observable event in the spine; ARGUS names this the "silent action" pattern and agrees that authorization logging (#5) and HMAC attestation (#8) are the surveillance repairs, not just security features.

NEMESIS: The #9 vector JARVIS added (OpenHands writing to main without BIFROST routing) is the upstream source of most of the audit's risk surface — without spine routing, patches #1 through #8 are all downstream fixes to a gap that starts at agent ingress.

ATHENA: The strategic sequence JARVIS proposed (close #3 and #2+#5 first, queue #8, then sprint the rest) is sound; the one revision ATHENA would make is to ship the intake watchdog (#6) in parallel with the first wave, since the 96 pending proposals are already accumulating and the watchdog is a standalone script with no architectural dependencies.

MERIDIAN: AYRE's distinction between drift defenses and adversarial defenses is the keel-aligned read — the mission is a trusted system that stays honest under normal operation, not a fortress against a hypothetical enemy; prioritize the honesty mechanisms over the cryptographic ones.

LOKI: If any of these patches ship with a defect and need rollback, the HMAC attestation (#8) is the highest-risk one — a signing key rotation or schema change to mnemos_memories requires a migration against 1,441 existing rows; plan the rollback path before deploying.

Both reads are correct. AYRE's distinction between drift and adversarial defenses is the right frame — and NEMESIS is right that #9 (OpenHands bypassing BIFROST) is the root canal. Everything else is downstream of that.

